-- title:
--   The law $P_\mu$ of a Markov process on $\mathbb D(\mathbf X)$
-- statement:
--   Let $P_\mu$ denote the distribution of the Markov process with initial distribution $\mu$ and transition semigroup $\{\mathcal P_t\}$ on $(\mathbb D(\mathbf X),\mathcal B(\mathbb D(\mathbf X)))$.
--
--   These are the two measures that an asymptotic coupling connects.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The Markov property is stated in integrated form on the coordinate $\sigma$-algebra up to time $s$.
-- source:
--   Lipshutz, Ramanan, Sensitivity analysis for the stationary distribution of RBM, arXiv:1907.00472v2, https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.00472v2, Theorem 8.1, p.33

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RBMSensitivity_Cadlag
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped NNReal ENNReal RealInnerProductSpace

namespace RBMSensitivity

/-- `m` is the distribution `P_μ` on `D(S)` of the Markov process with initial distribution `μ` and
transition semigroup `Pt`: arXiv:1907.00472v2, Theorem 8.1, p.33.

**Formalization Note.** The Markov property is in integrated form: for `A` in the σ-algebra generated by
the coordinates up to time `s` and Borel `C`,
`m(A ∩ {ζ(s + t) ∈ C}) = ∫_A P_t(ζ(s), C) m(dζ)`. The law need not exist for a given semigroup, and
the theorem is stated for laws that do. -/
def IsMarkovLaw {S : Type*} [TopologicalSpace S] [MeasurableSpace S] (Pt : ℝ≥0 → Kernel S S)
    (μ : Measure S) (m : Measure (Cadlag S)) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure m ∧ (∀ C, MeasurableSet C → m {ζ | ζ.1 0 ∈ C} = μ C) ∧
    ∀ (s t : ℝ≥0) (A : Set (Cadlag S)) (C : Set S),
      MeasurableSet[⨆ (u : ℝ≥0) (_ : u ≤ s),
        MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ζ : Cadlag S => ζ.1 u) inferInstance] A →
      MeasurableSet C →
      m (A ∩ {ζ | ζ.1 (s + t) ∈ C}) = ∫⁻ ζ in A, Pt t (ζ.1 s) C ∂m

end RBMSensitivity


